# DSS Code Prime - THE MUTATION ARMS REGISTRY: every declared demonstration that a mechanism, switched off, makes
# a test go RED. One arm, one block of rows. `dssharness check-mutations` reads this file and its grammar is the
# tool's (`dssharness help mutations`); the how-to, with a worked arm per class, is
# `.claude/skills/dss-cycle/references/mutation-arms.md`. This header states what is this file's own.
#
# ==============================================================================================================
# WHAT AN ARM IS HERE
# ==============================================================================================================
#
# AN ARM'S CLAIM IS A gtest TEST IN A SELF-CONTAINED TEST BINARY, and that binary's target is the arm's runner.
# The tool starts the runner's binary WHOLE, with its report arguments and nothing else, in a worker copy's root.
# An `examples/` or `integrated_tests/` entry is a corpus check the gate runs and never an arm's pin: a mutation
# only an example notices has no arm until a unit-tier TEST claims its mechanism.
#
# ==============================================================================================================
# THE STANDING CONDITIONS - each enforced, by the tool or by this layout, rather than restated in a row
# ==============================================================================================================
#
#   (a) EVERY TEST-RED ARM NAMES THE DIAGNOSTIC IT MUST PRODUCE - a `diag` file, written from an OBSERVED run -
#       and a red that does not say it is a violation, not a pass. A BUILD-RED arm matches NO compiler text:
#       its proof is the PAIRED POSITIVE CONTROL (`B`), the same construct with one value substituted, which
#       must build.
#   (b) AN ARM THAT PRODUCES NO RED IS `survived`. THERE IS NO ROW, FIELD OR TOKEN IN THIS GRAMMAR THAT CAN
#       DECLARE AN ARM INERT, and none is ever added: it would be an escape built into the mechanism whose
#       whole purpose is to close one.
#   (c) AN UNREGISTERED MUTATION IS DRIVEN BY NOBODY. Nothing is swept but an `A` row; a row naming an arm no
#       `A` row declares is refused; a text file under texts/ that no row cites is refused.
#   (d) A COUNT IS DERIVED, NEVER TYPED. How many arms: `grep -c '^A | ' tests/mutations/arms.registry`. No
#       figure is written into these comments.
#   (e) EVERY ARM HERE MUST READ `passed`. One arm that does not fails the sweep of its leg, and a runner that
#       is red UNMUTATED fails every arm that runs it. A mutation the verb cannot drive (the how-to names each
#       class, with its measurement) has NO row here - never a failing one.
#   (f) EVERY `C` ROW SAYS HOW ITS RED WAS MEASURED: the sweep that read it, and the hand reading before it
#       where one exists. A red predicted from the code and never observed is not a `C` row.
#   (g) EVERY MUTATION TEXT AND EVERY DIAGNOSTIC IS A FILE under tests/mutations/texts/. The grammar has no
#       escape, and the text a field would hold is the kind that holds a pipe.
#
# ==============================================================================================================
# LAYOUT - one file many hands append to, so it is laid out for clean merges
# ==============================================================================================================
#
#   * ONE SECTION PER AREA of the compiler - the first directory of the arm's site under `src/` (a site outside
#     `src/` goes to `tests`) - in alphabetical order, each opened by its own comment block. Never a section per
#     author, lane or cycle: none of those is a fact of the tree.
#   * AN ARM'S ID is `<area>-<what the mutation makes the code do>`, lowercase words joined by hyphens, at most
#     80 characters. Where several test binaries read ONE mutation, each is an arm of its own, `<that id>_<pin>`.
#   * AN ARM IS A BLOCK: its A row, then its M, B, C, G and S rows, then ONE blank line. Inside a section the
#     blocks are sorted by arm id, so two additions meet only where they sort together.
#   * THE A ROW'S WHY OPENS `CLAIM AT <test file>, TEST <Case>.` - the test that is the claim - and then says
#     what the mutation switches off and what would be true if nothing noticed. A BUILD-RED arm's opens
#     `CLAIM AT <file>, <the construct that refuses to compile>.` Never a line number.
#   * EVERY ARM CARRIES ONE `S` ROW NAMING ONE LEG, and its why says which rule chose it: (1) a claim about a
#     platform, a format or a toolchain names a leg of THAT platform - a second leg only where the claim is
#     about two; (2) any other claim names its AREA'S DEFAULT LEG, stated in the section's comment block. An
#     arm with no `S` row is refused by this convention though the tool would run it on every leg: the gate's
#     own runs prove a claim GREEN on every leg, and that it can go RED is shown once.
#   * A TEXT FILE is `texts/<the arm's id, up to its `_`>.<role>`: `before`, `after`, `control-before`,
#     `control-after`, and `2.before` / `2.after` for an M row's site (`3.` for the next); a `diag` that belongs
#     to one arm of several takes that arm's whole id. One flat directory, every file cited by its path from
#     the repository root. A text holds exactly the text and one line ending (UTF-8, LF). This file is ASCII.
#   * A HAND MERGE of this file (a tree's copy, the main tree's, their common base) is `git merge-file --union`
#     on copies, the blocks sorted again, and the result VALIDATED before anything else reads it.
#
# A | arm | site | before | after | red kind | target | runner | cases | diag | why
# C | arm | case | why          G | arm | case | why          S | arm | leg | why
# M | arm | site | before | after | why                       B | arm | control before | control after | why

# ==============================================================================================================
# analysis -- sites under src/analysis/ -- default leg: linux-x86_64-debug
# ==============================================================================================================

